The abstract setting for the situation discussed in Example 1 is to find maxima or minima of a function f(x₁, x₂,..,x (sub(n))) subject ot the constraint that g(x₁, x₂, ..., x(sub(n))) = c for some function g and constant c. 
(In Example 1, the function f is A(x, y, z), and the constraint is xyz = 4)
One method for finding constrained critical points is used implicityly in Example 1: Use the constrain equation g(x) = c to solve for one of the variables in terms of the others. 
Then substitute for this variable in the expression for f(x), thereby creating a new functino of one fewer variables. 
THis new function can then be maximized or minimized using techniques of 4.2. 
The theory, this is an entirely appropriate way to approcah such problems, but in practice there is one major drawback: It may be impossible to solve explicitly for any one of the variables in terms of the others. 
For example, you might wish to maximize

		f(x, y, z) = x² + 3y² + y²z⁴

subject to 

		g(x, y, z) = e^(xy) - x⁵y²z + cos(x/yz) = 2

There is no means of isolating any of x, y, or z on one side of the constraint equation, and so it is impossible for us to proceed any further along the lines of Example 1. 
